WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.660
Phillip: There are many advantages to working
in teams but there are also disadvantages.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.219
Being aware of both of these, can make
it easier for you to consider, how to

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.940
approach different projects and tasks.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.820
Maddy: In a team leadership context, knowing
the disadvantages you tend to find in

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.639
teams, will help you mitigate their effects.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.600
And knowing the advantages will
help you act to reinforce them.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.000
So, let’s start by looking at the Advantages

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:32.170
Phillip: Productivity – Teams tend to
be more productive than individuals.

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:35.679
This is because a team has a
wide pool of individual talents,

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.380
experience, and knowledge to draw from.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.820
And with more pairs of hands, more work
can get done in a shorter amount of time,

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.390
if tasks are appropriately allocated.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:52.079
Maddy: Ideas – In a team, ideas should
be shared frequently and openly.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:57.180
This not only allows everyone to have their
say, but it also exposes them to ideas

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.940
they might not otherwise have considered.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:04.390
Phillip: Belonging – In a team, you’re
not just one individual on your own,

00:01:04.789 --> 00:01:07.969
you are part of a group of people
working together, for the same goal.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:13.720
This can create a powerful sense of belonging,
which is good for people’s wellbeing and morale.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:19.250
Maddy: Mentorship – Teams give people
access to mentors who can support and

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:20.960
guide them on their career pathway.

00:01:21.559 --> 00:01:23.600
This can be official or unofficial.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:29.910
Phillip: Networking and relationships –In a team,
you’re dealing with a wider group of people, this

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.500
increases your professional and social circle.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:36.360
This can also lead to unexpected
development opportunities, you might

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:37.780
not otherwise have encountered.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:41.089
Maddy: So, now let’s look at the Disadvantages

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:46.489
Loss of individuality – For a team
to function properly, everyone

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.019
needs to put the team’s needs first.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.990
What this means in practice can be variable, but
it can result in some team members feeling like

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.909
they’ve lost their autonomy and individuality,
by not being able to prioritise themselves,

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.289
their ambitions, their desires, and their ideas.

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:09.530
Phillip: Groupthink – Teams need to have
consensus to function and that can sometimes

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:11.770
mean teams tend towards groupthink.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:17.710
That’s a situation where new ideas don’t get a
hearing because “That’s not how we do things”.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:22.970
In a situation like that, the team has
become too inward looking and conservative.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.530
Maddy: Cliques – Because teams often
become quite tightknit, this can leave

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:31.330
people not in the team, feeling left out.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:36.270
The team may even come to think of
itself as superior to “outsiders”, and

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:40.989
sometimes cliques can form within the team,
making some team members feel alienated.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.100
Phillip: Competition with other teams
– There’s nothing wrong with a bit of healthy

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.789
competition between teams in a workplace,
in fact, workplaces often encourage it.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:56.570
But it can go too far, and lead to
teams prioritising themselves, over the

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:58.519
strategic goals of the organisation.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:03.520
Maddy: Inequality in the team – although
everyone in an effective team should

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.830
be collaborating and working together,
inevitable situations will arise where some

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:10.500
people are given more to do than others.

00:03:11.179 --> 00:03:15.929
This can be for a lot of reasons such as
scheduling, professional experience, and so on.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.299
It can produce problems if some members
of the team start to perceive this as

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.590
favouritism or that their willingness
to work is being taken advantage of.

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:30.640
Phillip: On the whole teams have very strong
advantages, and will often outperform less

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.830
closely organised groups, but we can’t ignore
the disadvantages that are inherent in teams.

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:41.549
But we shouldn’t consider these disadvantages
as “failures” of teams, they inevitably

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:45.760
occur every now and again, because they
are totally reflective of human nature.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:51.000
What is important though, is that we are
realistic in our understanding of the

00:03:51.009 --> 00:04:02.560
problems, that even the most effective
teams will eventually have to deal with.

